<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cirencester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Corinium Dobunnorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cirencester is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinium-dobunnorum">Corinium Dobunnorum</a> it was the civitas capital of the Dobunni and the second-largest town of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it grew rich on the wool trade, which paid for its large parish church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cirencester.jpg" alt="Cirencester" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davecrosby uk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castle_Street_Cirencester.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
